1
00:00:01,709 --> 00:00:02,752
"Previously on The Mentalist..."

2
00:00:03,044 --> 00:00:05,046
- Hello. You were watching me.
- Hi. Yes.

3
00:00:05,171 --> 00:00:06,214
- Why?
- It's my job.

4
00:00:06,339 --> 00:00:09,091
That was Erica Flynn.
She wants to help out with the investigation.

5
00:00:09,342 --> 00:00:11,844
Great. We should fax over the case file
and get her a gun.

6
00:00:11,969 --> 00:00:14,472
"You're skilled at getting people
to open up to you, to trust you"

7
00:00:14,597 --> 00:00:16,224
and there's a question
whether you use that skill

8
00:00:16,349 --> 00:00:19,060
to help people
or to manipulate them.

9
00:00:19,352 --> 00:00:21,604
"You can't find happiness again,
even true love."

10
00:00:21,729 --> 00:00:23,231
You have to be willing
to let me help you.

11
00:00:23,356 --> 00:00:26,400
- Did you know Erica was gonna do this?
- Try to escape? Of course.

12
00:00:26,526 --> 00:00:27,902
I know you have feelings for me.

13
00:00:33,908 --> 00:00:36,827
This was a car bombing in Pakistan
14 months ago.

14
00:00:36,953 --> 00:00:39,372
7 people died, 30 injured.

15
00:00:40,915 --> 00:00:43,543
Egypt, eight months ago.
14 people killed.

16
00:00:45,169 --> 00:00:48,005
This was an attack on a hotel in Indonesia.

17
00:00:48,881 --> 00:00:54,887
{\an8}All of these are made possible by this man...
Jan Nemec. Slovakian.

18
00:00:55,221 --> 00:00:57,640
He was trained by
the Russian mob as a teenager.

19
00:00:57,890 --> 00:00:58,933
He's based in Beirut now.

20
00:00:59,058 --> 00:01:01,686
- So, he's part of a terrorist group?
- Not with a face like that.

21
00:01:01,894 --> 00:01:04,188
- He's obviously in it for the money.
- Correct.

22
00:01:04,397 --> 00:01:06,649
This is Danitra Cass.
She's C.I.A.

23
00:01:06,774 --> 00:01:08,818
They're here to ask for your help
with the Nemec case.

24
00:01:09,110 --> 00:01:12,363
Nemec's a supplier.
He sells weapons, explosives.

25
00:01:12,863 --> 00:01:15,533
We've been struggling
to get solid intelligence on him for years.

26
00:01:15,783 --> 00:01:18,828
But he's careful. Won't use computers,
doesn't leave a data trail.

27
00:01:19,078 --> 00:01:20,705
Well, you must have something
or you wouldn't be here.

28
00:01:21,038 --> 00:01:22,623
A source in Beirut
claims that Nemec

29
00:01:22,748 --> 00:01:25,459
just sent a shipment
of passport chips to the U.S.

30
00:01:25,710 --> 00:01:27,795
You use these chips
to forge passports

31
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:30,548
to get people in and out
of the country undetected.

32
00:01:30,881 --> 00:01:33,843
Our informant says that
he was paid 1.5 million Euros

33
00:01:33,968 --> 00:01:35,803
by a jihadist group
in the Philippines.

34
00:01:36,178 --> 00:01:39,265
We think it's the first step
towards an attack on American soil.

35
00:01:39,765 --> 00:01:43,102
We need to find these passport chips
and arrest Nemec

36
00:01:43,227 --> 00:01:44,895
and that is why we're asking
for your help.

37
00:01:45,438 --> 00:01:47,064
Actually, it's because of this woman.

38
00:01:49,692 --> 00:01:52,153
- Erica Flynn?
- I understand you've dealt with her.

39
00:01:52,361 --> 00:01:56,324
She ran a dating service in California.
We arrested her for killing her husband.

40
00:01:56,657 --> 00:01:59,619
She escaped from prison about a year ago.
She's been on the run ever since.

41
00:01:59,910 --> 00:02:02,747
{\an8}Well, she ran into Nemec.
She's his girlfriend.

42
00:02:03,372 --> 00:02:05,958
What exactly was your relationship
with her, Mr. Jane?

43
00:02:06,334 --> 00:02:08,961
- Me? Why?
- Because we contacted Ms. Flynn

44
00:02:09,086 --> 00:02:12,798
and she's agreed to help us
find the chips, arrest Nemec

45
00:02:12,965 --> 00:02:15,593
in exchange for a reduced sentence
of her murder charge.

46
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:20,097
But she insists on working with you.
Says no other person will do.

47
00:02:22,058 --> 00:02:24,852
- Excuse me.
- No other person will do.

48
00:02:25,478 --> 00:02:26,520
Don't ask me.

49
00:02:26,687 --> 00:02:28,689
Abbott, we got the link set up
in your office.

50
00:02:28,814 --> 00:02:30,941
Okay, let's go.
Join us, please.

51
00:02:31,776 --> 00:02:34,570
Erica Flynn's not asking to work with me.
Why am I a part of this?

52
00:02:34,904 --> 00:02:36,113
Well, if Jane goes to Beirut

53
00:02:36,238 --> 00:02:38,115
he's gonna need a partner
to go undercover with him.

54
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:40,701
You've done it before.
Seemed like a logical choice.

55
00:02:40,910 --> 00:02:42,078
- Is there a problem?
- I arrested her.

56
00:02:42,203 --> 00:02:44,246
I don't know how I feel
about helping her avoid prison.

57
00:02:44,372 --> 00:02:46,332
Yeah, it's a... a tradeoff. I know

58
00:02:46,457 --> 00:02:49,960
but it's worth it, I think,
with the issues at hand.

59
00:02:50,127 --> 00:02:53,047
- Jane? Good.
- Me? No. No problem.

60
00:02:53,798 --> 00:02:57,843
Well, Lisbon, if you still have concerns
we can discuss it after the meeting, okay?

61
00:03:09,271 --> 00:03:10,398
Here we go.

62
00:03:13,567 --> 00:03:15,736
Ms. Flynn,
everyone's here.

63
00:03:16,028 --> 00:03:19,657
"Patrick. It's so good to see you.
And Agent Lisbon."

64
00:03:20,116 --> 00:03:21,158
Hello.

65
00:03:22,118 --> 00:03:24,870
- What is this about, Erica?
- "Isn't it obvious?"

66
00:03:25,663 --> 00:03:27,248
- "I need you."
- Really?

67
00:03:27,456 --> 00:03:30,793
"Jan uses a courier to send messages
but they're coded."

68
00:03:31,460 --> 00:03:33,337
"The courier has to memorize
a long string of numbers."

69
00:03:33,462 --> 00:03:35,756
"I can't think of anyone with a better
memory than yours."

70
00:03:36,507 --> 00:03:39,760
Nemec's courier was killed in an accident.
He's looking for a new one.

71
00:03:39,969 --> 00:03:42,054
He has to let the people
that bought the passport chips

72
00:03:42,179 --> 00:03:43,222
know where to pick them up.

73
00:03:44,056 --> 00:03:46,350
We'd like you to go to Beirut
and be the courier.

74
00:03:46,892 --> 00:03:49,729
He'll give you the code, we'll crack it
get the chips, and him.

75
00:03:51,021 --> 00:03:52,565
Well, I guess I could do that.

76
00:03:53,607 --> 00:03:54,650
"Wonderful."

77
00:03:56,152 --> 00:03:57,194
"See you when you get here."

78
00:03:57,820 --> 00:03:59,780
"I look forward to working
with you again, Patrick."

79
00:04:01,490 --> 00:04:02,950
Interesting woman.

80
00:04:03,367 --> 00:04:06,912
Sir? There's no need to meet.
It's an important mission.

81
00:04:07,037 --> 00:04:08,873
I'm happy to be a part of it.
It's no problem.

82
00:04:09,206 --> 00:04:10,458
- No?
- None at all.

83
00:04:10,958 --> 00:04:12,960
- So, we're all good, then?
- Yeah, we're good.

84
00:05:08,430 --> 00:05:09,473
{\an8}Thank you.

85
00:05:09,598 --> 00:05:12,267
{\an8}You know what they have here?
Great Orange blossom ice cream.

86
00:05:12,392 --> 00:05:14,269
{\an8}- Oh, that sounds good.
- Yeah, we'll get some.

87
00:05:28,659 --> 00:05:30,536
{\an8}- Here we are.
- Here we go.

88
00:05:34,957 --> 00:05:36,250
Patrick. Welcome.

89
00:05:37,209 --> 00:05:38,585
Teresa, it's so good to see you.

90
00:05:39,002 --> 00:05:41,421
- Hi.
- Erica. I was wondering if you'd be here.

91
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:43,298
I thought we should talk
as soon as you got in.

92
00:05:43,423 --> 00:05:45,175
{\an8}- I hope you don't mind.
- Not at all.

93
00:05:45,467 --> 00:05:46,760
{\an8}I had them prepare tea.

94
00:05:48,303 --> 00:05:49,346
So thoughtful.

95
00:05:51,431 --> 00:05:53,559
{\an8}- Teresa, may I?
- No, thanks.

96
00:05:54,768 --> 00:05:57,896
{\an8}So, the FBI.
That's quite an accomplishment.

97
00:05:58,063 --> 00:06:00,858
Ms. Flynn, if you don't mind
I'd like to ask you a couple questions.

98
00:06:01,108 --> 00:06:03,402
Of course.
Erica, please.

99
00:06:03,569 --> 00:06:05,445
How did you get involved
with Jan Nemec?

100
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:08,240
{\an8}It's kind of embarrassing, actually.

101
00:06:08,448 --> 00:06:12,035
{\an8}I ran into some trouble in Sao Paulo...
money trouble.

102
00:06:12,244 --> 00:06:14,538
You're in a foreign country
cut off from everything you know.

103
00:06:14,705 --> 00:06:16,665
You know how difficult it is
to live like that.

104
00:06:16,957 --> 00:06:18,542
Well, I think you were born
to live like that.

105
00:06:18,917 --> 00:06:19,960
You flatter me.

106
00:06:20,961 --> 00:06:23,630
Jan was very charming,
and he wanted to help.

107
00:06:24,047 --> 00:06:26,967
{\an8}We started seeing each other,
fell into traveling together.

108
00:06:27,843 --> 00:06:31,847
{\an8}I didn't know what he does for a living.
He told me he was a businessman.

109
00:06:32,222 --> 00:06:35,684
- And you believed him?
- He's very secretive about his work.

110
00:06:36,685 --> 00:06:42,024
{\an8}I suspected it was unsavory, maybe illegal
but not what it actually involves.

111
00:06:42,149 --> 00:06:43,901
{\an8}When the C.I.A. told me,
I was horrified.

112
00:06:44,234 --> 00:06:47,362
{\an8}And that's why you're turning him in...
because he horrifies you?

113
00:06:48,196 --> 00:06:52,034
{\an8}No. Mainly because
I wanna go back home.

114
00:06:53,201 --> 00:06:55,913
{\an8}- I miss it more than I thought I would.
- You're homesick.

115
00:06:56,330 --> 00:06:57,372
Is that so strange?

116
00:06:57,497 --> 00:06:59,583
{\an8}When you were away,
weren't there things that you missed

117
00:07:00,083 --> 00:07:01,835
{\an8}that you would have given
anything to get back?

118
00:07:02,169 --> 00:07:03,879
Yeah. There... there were.

119
00:07:07,716 --> 00:07:10,010
{\an8}I can see you don't
believe me, Teresa.

120
00:07:10,636 --> 00:07:13,013
{\an8}I just hope that in time
you'll see that I'm telling the truth.

121
00:07:13,847 --> 00:07:15,432
We should go see Jan.
He's expecting you.

122
00:07:15,849 --> 00:07:18,101
- Okay.
- Patrick has to go alone.

123
00:07:18,435 --> 00:07:21,021
{\an8}He's very suspicious.
He doesn't even know you're here.

124
00:07:21,897 --> 00:07:24,274
- I don't think I like this.
- Don't worry. It'll... it'll be fine.

125
00:07:25,025 --> 00:07:27,194
- Lead the way.
- Excuse me.

126
00:07:31,365 --> 00:07:34,451
So, you and Lisbon are together,
as in a couple?

127
00:07:35,786 --> 00:07:37,079
{\an8}I can tell by the way
she looks at you

128
00:07:37,204 --> 00:07:39,706
that you have more than
a professional relationship.

129
00:07:40,832 --> 00:07:42,542
That is, in fact, correct, yes.

130
00:07:44,127 --> 00:07:45,671
- I'm happy for you, Patrick.
- Thank you.

131
00:07:46,046 --> 00:07:48,882
For both of you.
But especially for you.

132
00:07:49,716 --> 00:07:52,928
I know how much you wanted love in your life.
I'm very happy you found it.

133
00:07:54,137 --> 00:07:55,514
What is this really about, Erica?

134
00:07:55,973 --> 00:07:58,475
- What do you mean?
- Why are you doing this?

135
00:07:59,393 --> 00:08:01,061
I told you. I wanna go home.

136
00:08:01,603 --> 00:08:03,021
Yes, but there's something else.

137
00:08:04,690 --> 00:08:07,484
You're wrong.
But thank you for suspecting me.

138
00:08:08,068 --> 00:08:09,820
Makes me feel very interesting.

139
00:08:11,405 --> 00:08:13,156
These men will take you to Jan.

140
00:08:15,784 --> 00:08:17,661
- Yes.
- Come.

141
00:08:20,455 --> 00:08:24,543
- Guess I'll see you a little later.
- Good luck, Patrick.

142
00:10:00,686 --> 00:10:02,897
Hi. I'm here to see Jan Nemec.

143
00:10:12,865 --> 00:10:15,701
Where are we going?
I don't need to go to the bathroom.

144
00:10:17,161 --> 00:10:18,204
Okay.

145
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:23,042
What... what is this?

146
00:10:23,167 --> 00:10:25,920
What is... what is this,
all about here?

147
00:10:27,004 --> 00:10:28,631
Listen guys, if you were
planning on scaring me

148
00:10:28,756 --> 00:10:30,800
then mission accomplished.
I am... I'm scared.

149
00:10:30,967 --> 00:10:32,635
Am I... am I gonna get to see
Mr. Nemec?

150
00:10:32,844 --> 00:10:34,137
- Listen.
- I'm listening.

151
00:10:34,554 --> 00:10:38,808
Listen. Helicopter.
Electric range. Newfoundland.

152
00:10:38,933 --> 00:10:41,477
Albino. Retrovirus.
Wheelbarrow.

153
00:10:47,859 --> 00:10:49,444
Okay, what... what...

154
00:10:49,986 --> 00:10:54,323
Battery. Tractor. Fox.
Hypotenuse. Slender. Elm.

155
00:11:02,373 --> 00:11:04,167
Stop! Stop, please. Stop.

156
00:11:04,292 --> 00:11:07,795
Stone. Harmony.
Lint. Banana. Ladder.

157
00:11:25,813 --> 00:11:27,815
Okay. Okay.

158
00:11:42,788 --> 00:11:44,040
Patrick Jane, right?

159
00:11:46,167 --> 00:11:48,878
Jan. Jan Nemec.

160
00:11:50,630 --> 00:11:52,548
Sorry. That was pretty nasty, I know.

161
00:11:52,673 --> 00:11:57,720
Still, I need you to repeat the words
he just said to you in order.

162
00:11:58,387 --> 00:11:59,430
A towel.

163
00:12:00,765 --> 00:12:03,601
- Yeah, sure. First the words.
- I want a towel.

164
00:12:11,734 --> 00:12:12,777
Thank you.

165
00:12:14,987 --> 00:12:18,824
Helicopter. Electric range.
Newfoundland.

166
00:12:18,950 --> 00:12:22,328
Albino. Retrovirus.
Wheelbarrow. Battery.

167
00:12:23,246 --> 00:12:25,665
Tractor.
Fox. Hypotenuse.

168
00:12:26,123 --> 00:12:28,584
Slender. Elm. Stone.

169
00:12:29,335 --> 00:12:32,338
Harmony. Lint. Banana.

170
00:12:34,215 --> 00:12:35,258
Ladder.

171
00:12:35,383 --> 00:12:37,468
Erica said your memory was good.
She's right.

172
00:12:37,760 --> 00:12:39,762
- Just a trick.
- No, I tested others.

173
00:12:39,887 --> 00:12:41,931
You're the only one
getting everything correct.

174
00:12:42,139 --> 00:12:46,519
- I didn't say it was an easy trick.
- Like I said, uh, I'm sorry.

175
00:12:46,894 --> 00:12:47,937
Please.

176
00:12:49,564 --> 00:12:50,982
Let's have something to drink.

177
00:12:52,149 --> 00:12:56,112
- I'd rather talk about the job.
- Nah. You don't want to have a drink?

178
00:12:57,113 --> 00:12:59,615
What kind of a man are you, Jane?

179
00:13:03,578 --> 00:13:04,787
Okay. The job.

180
00:13:05,246 --> 00:13:07,748
I need you to carry
a message up here.

181
00:13:09,458 --> 00:13:12,211
- What kind of message?
- 81 unrelated numbers.

182
00:13:12,336 --> 00:13:14,839
You'll have to memorize them.
They cannot be written down.

183
00:13:14,964 --> 00:13:17,883
When you get to the other end
you'll have to repeat them in precise order.

184
00:13:18,009 --> 00:13:20,720
- Otherwise, it's useless.
- What does it mean? What's it about?

185
00:13:20,845 --> 00:13:22,638
Oh, that's exactly the point.

186
00:13:23,681 --> 00:13:24,724
You don't know.

187
00:13:25,516 --> 00:13:28,477
- What are you paying?
- 10,000 Euros.

188
00:13:29,270 --> 00:13:30,730
If it works out, there's gonna be more.

189
00:13:30,855 --> 00:13:32,231
- 15,000.
- 10,000.

190
00:13:32,440 --> 00:13:34,692
20,000, just for getting me wet.

191
00:13:37,320 --> 00:13:40,239
- 10,000.
- Get someone else. I'm not interested.

192
00:13:48,372 --> 00:13:50,207
Erica said you were an actor.

193
00:13:52,877 --> 00:13:54,503
- Sort of.
- And a policeman?

194
00:13:54,920 --> 00:13:56,714
Yeah, I worked with the police.

195
00:13:57,632 --> 00:14:00,593
There was something I wanted.
And when I got it, I left.

196
00:14:00,801 --> 00:14:04,805
- Because you killed someone, she said.
- More than one... Actually.

197
00:14:05,848 --> 00:14:06,891
But who's counting?

198
00:14:07,141 --> 00:14:10,728
You know, if you want to check me
or look me up it's on the Internet.

199
00:14:11,103 --> 00:14:13,898
Oh, we have... Patrick.

200
00:14:15,316 --> 00:14:16,359
We have.

201
00:14:17,818 --> 00:14:20,279
Relax. Everything looks okay.

202
00:14:21,447 --> 00:14:24,325
Erica says you're a good guy.
It's all good.

203
00:14:25,993 --> 00:14:27,828
I have only one question, though.

204
00:14:30,289 --> 00:14:31,332
Who's this?

205
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,836
And, why is she with you?

206
00:14:37,546 --> 00:14:38,589
Hi.

207
00:14:55,988 --> 00:15:00,909
- Why are you wet?
- That's a little... gag of Jan's here.

208
00:15:01,451 --> 00:15:04,162
Jan Nemec, this is my girlfriend,
Teresa Lisbon.

209
00:15:04,621 --> 00:15:06,081
We traveled together.

210
00:15:06,665 --> 00:15:09,626
So, uh... What are you doing
in Beirut?

211
00:15:10,419 --> 00:15:13,005
- Patrick thought it would be a good idea.
- She has nothing to do with this.

212
00:15:13,130 --> 00:15:16,216
- Could she just go back to the hotel please?
- You can ask me anything.

213
00:15:16,925 --> 00:15:20,178
Well, maybe you can tell me
if you're with the FBI.

214
00:15:20,846 --> 00:15:23,056
- No. Why?
- What?

215
00:15:23,348 --> 00:15:28,770
Well, it says here, FBI website
there's a Special Agent Teresa Lisbon.

216
00:15:28,895 --> 00:15:31,523
It's a mistake. That's crazy.
Check again.

217
00:15:33,608 --> 00:15:34,651
What's that?

218
00:15:34,776 --> 00:15:36,695
Someone's searching for Lisbon
in an FBI database.

219
00:15:36,820 --> 00:15:39,531
- Is that a problem.
- No, we're set up for it.

220
00:15:51,001 --> 00:15:52,961
- Different woman.
- Told you.

221
00:15:53,420 --> 00:15:55,839
- Yeah.
- Sorry. Mistake.

222
00:15:56,548 --> 00:15:59,968
- It's all right. No harm done.
- Yeah, well, we're going back to the hotel.

223
00:16:00,093 --> 00:16:02,763
If you need to talk to me,
you know where to find me.

224
00:16:14,399 --> 00:16:15,484
Metropole hotel.

225
00:16:27,079 --> 00:16:29,623
Wylie suggested building
the fake profile.

226
00:16:30,290 --> 00:16:33,919
"So, now when they see any other
mention of Teresa Lisbon"

227
00:16:35,128 --> 00:16:36,379
"they'll assume it's that woman."

228
00:16:36,880 --> 00:16:38,381
Tell Wylie I owe him one.

229
00:16:39,049 --> 00:16:40,175
"So, what do you think?"

230
00:16:40,509 --> 00:16:42,511
- Is Nemec on the hook?
- It's hard to tell.

231
00:16:42,928 --> 00:16:45,222
Jane made an impression.
That we know.

232
00:16:48,809 --> 00:16:49,851
We'll get back to you.

233
00:16:58,485 --> 00:16:59,903
- Are you all right?
- Fine.

234
00:17:00,487 --> 00:17:01,947
You have a lot of nerve
showing up here.

235
00:17:02,197 --> 00:17:04,366
- What do you mean?
- You told Nemec about me.

236
00:17:04,699 --> 00:17:06,618
It was you. You're the only one
who knows I'm here.

237
00:17:06,868 --> 00:17:08,453
- Of course I did.
- Why?

238
00:17:08,954 --> 00:17:11,790
- She's trying to gain Nemec's trust.
- Yes.

239
00:17:13,458 --> 00:17:15,210
I've been trying to convince Jan
to hire Patrick.

240
00:17:15,335 --> 00:17:17,587
He was suspicious,
so telling him about you

241
00:17:17,712 --> 00:17:19,172
only helped prove
that I'm trustworthy.

242
00:17:19,297 --> 00:17:22,551
- You should have told us.
- I'm sorry. I was working fast.

243
00:17:22,801 --> 00:17:23,885
I assumed you would be prepared

244
00:17:24,010 --> 00:17:25,679
for the possibility that
he would find out about you.

245
00:17:25,887 --> 00:17:29,266
- We are.
- Good. Because it worked.

246
00:17:29,641 --> 00:17:31,518
He's downstairs.
He's ready to make the deal.

247
00:17:32,894 --> 00:17:35,230
Do you mind if I stay up here?
He doesn't want me down there.

248
00:17:37,274 --> 00:17:38,316
Okay.

249
00:17:40,527 --> 00:17:41,570
I'll be going.

250
00:17:42,320 --> 00:17:43,363
Bye, y'all.

251
00:17:44,573 --> 00:17:45,991
Get a little room service.

252
00:18:02,674 --> 00:18:03,717
No, Patrick.

253
00:18:18,940 --> 00:18:22,777
Okay. 20,000.

254
00:18:23,653 --> 00:18:25,113
But I need you to go right away.

255
00:18:25,655 --> 00:18:28,408
5,000 now.
15,000 when you come back.

256
00:18:30,702 --> 00:18:33,830
- Where am I going?
- The U.S. Atlanta, Georgia.

257
00:18:34,956 --> 00:18:37,167
You need to go tonight,
tomorrow at the latest.

258
00:18:37,334 --> 00:18:38,752
When you get there,
call this number.

259
00:18:39,544 --> 00:18:42,172
Guy who answers
will say his name is John Paul.

260
00:18:42,297 --> 00:18:45,508
When he gives you that name,
you say "Dad sends his love."

261
00:18:46,051 --> 00:18:49,262
He'll say "I miss him." That means
everything is fine and you can meet.

262
00:18:49,512 --> 00:18:52,599
He'll tell you an address.
You go there, give him a message.

263
00:18:53,725 --> 00:18:54,768
Job done.

264
00:18:56,311 --> 00:18:57,354
What's the message?

265
00:19:01,233 --> 00:19:03,610
Left to right, top to bottom,
in order.

266
00:19:04,653 --> 00:19:06,529
- Can I have a few minutes?
- Yeah, sure.

267
00:19:19,292 --> 00:19:21,544
Erica says you're the smartest
man she ever met.

268
00:19:22,837 --> 00:19:23,880
Yeah.

269
00:19:24,923 --> 00:19:27,050
Never heard her say that
about anyone else.

270
00:19:33,139 --> 00:19:35,308
You had her, right?

271
00:19:38,311 --> 00:19:41,356
- I'm working.
- Of course.

272
00:19:54,494 --> 00:19:55,537
You speak Lebanese?

273
00:19:56,121 --> 00:19:57,956
Just enough to say
"please" and "thank you."

274
00:19:59,457 --> 00:20:00,917
- Would you like some?
- I'm good.

275
00:20:04,212 --> 00:20:05,922
I hope you don't mind me saying so

276
00:20:06,256 --> 00:20:08,883
but I'm very happy that you and Patrick
have found each other.

277
00:20:09,676 --> 00:20:12,762
Patrick likes to pretend
that he's this lone wolf.

278
00:20:13,471 --> 00:20:15,932
He's needed someone in his life
ever since his wife died.

279
00:20:16,308 --> 00:20:17,559
I'm very glad it's you.

280
00:20:18,101 --> 00:20:21,730
- Did he say something to you?
- I used to be a matchmaker.

281
00:20:22,731 --> 00:20:24,649
I can tell when someone
is starting a new relationship.

282
00:20:24,774 --> 00:20:26,276
It's a beautiful thing to see.

283
00:20:27,527 --> 00:20:29,779
Well... Thanks.

284
00:20:32,615 --> 00:20:34,951
I also sense some tension
between you and I.

285
00:20:35,702 --> 00:20:37,078
And I really don't want that.

286
00:20:38,455 --> 00:20:43,043
- You have nothing to worry about.
- Why would I worry? What?

287
00:20:43,376 --> 00:20:47,255
- Did Patrick not say anything?
- About what?

288
00:20:47,881 --> 00:20:51,259
I should have known.
So typical.

289
00:20:51,968 --> 00:20:54,179
Men like to pretend
like the past never happened.

290
00:20:54,679 --> 00:20:56,181
We're the ones who remember.

291
00:20:59,017 --> 00:21:01,728
I shouldn't have said anything.
Please forgive me.

292
00:21:08,276 --> 00:21:09,319
"54."

293
00:21:11,321 --> 00:21:12,364
10.

294
00:21:14,074 --> 00:21:15,116
300.

295
00:21:16,910 --> 00:21:17,952
"28."

296
00:21:19,120 --> 00:21:20,163
"6."

297
00:21:21,706 --> 00:21:22,749
That's 81 numbers.

298
00:21:23,291 --> 00:21:26,669
Mr. Jane, are you sure about these?
There's a lot hanging on this.

299
00:21:26,836 --> 00:21:29,506
- Yes, I'm sure.
- All right. We got it from here, buddy.

300
00:21:29,756 --> 00:21:32,258
You got a plane to catch tomorrow.
Lay low, okay?

301
00:21:32,384 --> 00:21:33,426
Will do.

302
00:21:36,388 --> 00:21:38,264
You know, I'm not that tired.
How about you?

303
00:21:38,598 --> 00:21:40,642
- No.
- Want to go get something to eat?

304
00:21:40,975 --> 00:21:43,978
Bellboy was telling me about this restaurant
that does a great "manakish".

305
00:21:44,270 --> 00:21:45,313
Yeah, sure.

306
00:21:48,691 --> 00:21:50,235
- Everything okay?
- Fine.

307
00:21:51,653 --> 00:21:52,695
Okay.

308
00:21:53,321 --> 00:21:57,158
Your liaison... give him the numbers
and any other support that they ask for.

309
00:21:58,034 --> 00:21:59,786
Tell Vega to stay close
and give you the help you need.

310
00:22:00,078 --> 00:22:01,121
Got it.

311
00:22:03,456 --> 00:22:05,333
- And what if they can't crack it?
- What are you thinking?

312
00:22:06,459 --> 00:22:07,585
This guy, John Paul

313
00:22:08,670 --> 00:22:10,713
does he have any idea when Jane's
supposed to arrive in the U.S.?

314
00:22:11,131 --> 00:22:14,050
No. And Nemec won't risk
getting in touch with him.

315
00:22:14,300 --> 00:22:17,429
Jane said he was only given a window
of arrival, not a specific date.

316
00:22:17,637 --> 00:22:19,305
So, as far as he knows,
Jane's already here.

317
00:22:19,848 --> 00:22:22,308
- Does he know what Jane looks like?
- No. He doesn't.

318
00:22:22,934 --> 00:22:23,977
So, let's set up the meet.

319
00:22:24,686 --> 00:22:27,355
We get the message to John Paul
we follow him to the location

320
00:22:27,772 --> 00:22:31,651
we grab him and the passport chips
even if he can't crack the code.

321
00:22:33,153 --> 00:22:35,905
- Is that okay with the C.I.A.?
- As long as we're in on it.

322
00:22:36,865 --> 00:22:37,907
Our pleasure.

323
00:22:43,371 --> 00:22:45,248
- This is John Paul.
- Dad sends his love.

324
00:22:45,540 --> 00:22:46,875
- I miss him.
- Where should I go?

325
00:22:47,125 --> 00:22:48,626
- You're in Atlanta?
- Yes.

326
00:22:48,877 --> 00:22:49,919
"How soon can you meet?"

327
00:22:52,422 --> 00:22:53,465
Five hours.

328
00:22:56,301 --> 00:22:58,178
There's a roadhouse
in Brookhaven.

329
00:23:00,096 --> 00:23:02,098
Little Ed's on Walthrop.

330
00:23:02,640 --> 00:23:04,809
- Turn your coffee cup upside down.
- Okay.

331
00:23:10,940 --> 00:23:12,025
Fire up the jet.

332
00:23:17,739 --> 00:23:21,075
So, anyway, the analysts are trying
something called modulo-2 arithmetic

333
00:23:21,201 --> 00:23:23,745
which sounds very cool.
I just wish I knew what it meant.

334
00:23:27,665 --> 00:23:28,708
Hello?

335
00:23:29,709 --> 00:23:32,629
- Sorry, uh, what were you saying?
- Nothing.

336
00:23:34,297 --> 00:23:36,132
Looks like they're getting ready
for the meet.

337
00:23:37,050 --> 00:23:38,092
Yep.

338
00:23:38,468 --> 00:23:40,553
- Pretty exciting, I guess.
- Yeah.

339
00:23:42,722 --> 00:23:45,141
Anyway, how can I help you here?

340
00:23:45,934 --> 00:23:47,519
You should go with Cho.
I don't need your help.

341
00:23:47,852 --> 00:23:48,895
- No.
- No, don't worry.

342
00:23:49,020 --> 00:23:50,063
It's fine. Don't worry about it.

343
00:23:50,188 --> 00:23:51,606
- You mean it?
- Yeah. Sure.

344
00:23:51,981 --> 00:23:54,526
- Thank you. You're the best.
- Of course.

345
00:23:55,068 --> 00:23:56,110
Sir.

346
00:23:57,153 --> 00:23:59,531
Agent Wylie doesn't need my help.
Okay if I come along with you?

347
00:24:00,073 --> 00:24:01,533
You run it by Abbott?
Did he okay it?

348
00:24:02,200 --> 00:24:04,577
- Yes, sir, he did.
- Suit up. We leave right away.

349
00:24:19,759 --> 00:24:22,262
Only two minutes past the target hour.
Everybody stay loose.

350
00:24:30,270 --> 00:24:31,938
Male, 30s, from the north.

351
00:24:34,065 --> 00:24:35,817
- There she is.
- Hey!

352
00:24:36,150 --> 00:24:37,193
False alarm.

353
00:24:52,834 --> 00:24:55,253
Another male.
Late 20s, possibly Filipino.

354
00:25:21,029 --> 00:25:23,573
Hey. Hey, everybody stay calm.

355
00:25:55,688 --> 00:25:56,773
You bitch!

356
00:25:59,108 --> 00:26:00,151
Vega!

357
00:26:03,446 --> 00:26:05,406
- You okay?
- Yeah.

358
00:26:24,163 --> 00:26:25,290
Oh, thank you.

359
00:26:29,586 --> 00:26:30,628
What?

360
00:26:31,754 --> 00:26:33,131
What was that thing
you said was good?

361
00:26:33,256 --> 00:26:34,841
- It was mana something?
- Manakish.

362
00:26:35,258 --> 00:26:37,802
Yeah, it's like a little pizza.

363
00:26:40,805 --> 00:26:42,765
- What's up?
- Nothing.

364
00:26:45,393 --> 00:26:46,436
Nothing.

365
00:26:52,317 --> 00:26:54,694
Did something happen
between you and Erica Flynn?

366
00:26:54,944 --> 00:26:55,987
Why?

367
00:26:56,154 --> 00:27:01,159
She made a point of letting me
know that it did, so did it?

368
00:27:04,787 --> 00:27:05,955
Well, um...

369
00:27:07,790 --> 00:27:10,251
Actually, yes.
Something did.

370
00:27:12,420 --> 00:27:14,505
When we were working
together, we... we kissed.

371
00:27:14,714 --> 00:27:17,091
- You kissed?
- Just once in her hotel room.

372
00:27:17,634 --> 00:27:20,094
- This story just keeps on getting better.
- It was nothing.

373
00:27:20,470 --> 00:27:22,972
- Don't say that, okay?
- Okay, well, you're right.

374
00:27:23,097 --> 00:27:26,309
It... it wasn't nothing at the time.

375
00:27:26,517 --> 00:27:29,312
But it was a long time ago,
and, um...

376
00:27:30,229 --> 00:27:33,149
It was never gonna amount to anything.
It just... it... it couldn't.

377
00:27:33,983 --> 00:27:36,319
- Why didn't you tell me?
- I didn't think about it.

378
00:27:36,736 --> 00:27:38,988
Well, when you found out we were coming here
you could have told me.

379
00:27:39,113 --> 00:27:43,576
Yes, I thought about it then
but I... I made a decision... not to tell you

380
00:27:43,701 --> 00:27:48,414
because I was worried that, um...

381
00:27:50,291 --> 00:27:52,001
That it would come between us.

382
00:27:52,543 --> 00:27:55,630
I just wish I'd heard it from you first.
That's all.

383
00:27:57,924 --> 00:27:58,967
I understand.

384
00:28:00,802 --> 00:28:04,472
Yeah, uh... I should have told you.

385
00:28:06,057 --> 00:28:07,850
- I'm sorry.
- It's okay.

386
00:28:08,851 --> 00:28:10,186
Really. It's okay.

387
00:28:15,108 --> 00:28:17,276
Are there any others
that I should know about?

388
00:28:17,485 --> 00:28:20,363
Like, people we've worked with? It would...
it would help me if I... if I knew.

389
00:28:20,488 --> 00:28:22,240
- No.
- Lorelei?

390
00:28:22,740 --> 00:28:24,909
I always thought that maybe
something happened with you two.

391
00:28:25,868 --> 00:28:26,911
Lorelei?

392
00:28:32,709 --> 00:28:33,751
Okay, how about this?

393
00:28:35,003 --> 00:28:36,295
I'll tell you about Lorelei

394
00:28:37,255 --> 00:28:39,507
if you would tell me
about one Walter Mashburn.

395
00:28:43,469 --> 00:28:45,471
You said that manakish
is like a little pizza?

396
00:28:45,596 --> 00:28:46,639
- Yeah, that's right.
- Yum.

397
00:28:46,806 --> 00:28:48,099
- Let's get some.
- Yeah.

398
00:28:49,100 --> 00:28:51,310
- Tea?
- Please.

399
00:28:53,271 --> 00:28:54,397
Is that your phone?

400
00:29:01,070 --> 00:29:02,739
- What's up, boss?
- "We've got a problem."

401
00:29:03,281 --> 00:29:05,241
"His full name
is John Paul Navarro."

402
00:29:05,575 --> 00:29:08,327
Looks like he was connected
to a jihadist group in the Philippines.

403
00:29:08,911 --> 00:29:11,956
- We're still looking into that.
- "What about the blank passport chips?"

404
00:29:12,582 --> 00:29:15,710
Well, looks like they're in the Atlanta area
but we just don't know where.

405
00:29:16,044 --> 00:29:17,879
How's the C.I.A. doing with the code?

406
00:29:18,254 --> 00:29:20,339
They can't crack it.
They think it's a one-time pad.

407
00:29:20,548 --> 00:29:23,676
- What's that?
- "It's a-a code made with a key."

408
00:29:23,801 --> 00:29:25,845
The person who encodes
the message has the key

409
00:29:25,970 --> 00:29:27,472
so does the person
who decodes it.

410
00:29:27,889 --> 00:29:29,223
Without it, you can't make sense of it.

411
00:29:29,807 --> 00:29:32,685
Nemec and John Paul must have the key.
Did you check John Paul's apartment?

412
00:29:33,019 --> 00:29:35,188
Yeah, but we need to know
what we're looking for.

413
00:29:35,313 --> 00:29:37,815
It could be anything...
a magazine, a book.

414
00:29:38,149 --> 00:29:39,859
- It's a book.
- "Why?"

415
00:29:40,068 --> 00:29:43,988
Well, Nemec has two bookshelves
filled, and he's not a reader.

416
00:29:44,322 --> 00:29:46,491
We still need to know
which book it is.

417
00:29:46,908 --> 00:29:49,077
So, in the meantime,
you two need to get out of there.

418
00:29:49,285 --> 00:29:52,872
Pretty soon, Nemec is gonna find out
that John Paul is dead, and when he does

419
00:29:53,289 --> 00:29:54,499
you two need to
stay out of sight.

420
00:29:54,707 --> 00:29:56,876
Change hotels, stay indoors

421
00:29:57,043 --> 00:29:59,087
"and get on that plane
tomorrow."

422
00:29:59,337 --> 00:30:00,671
Yeah, we'll definitely do all of that

423
00:30:00,797 --> 00:30:02,882
but I think we can find
those computer chips.

424
00:30:03,257 --> 00:30:05,468
- How?
- I'll tell you later. Bye.

425
00:30:06,761 --> 00:30:07,804
What's your plan?

426
00:30:07,970 --> 00:30:11,349
The key is probably in one of the books
in Nemec's apartment.

427
00:30:11,641 --> 00:30:12,934
I just need to figure out which one.

428
00:30:13,101 --> 00:30:14,477
Well, how are we gonna
get into the apartment?

429
00:30:14,602 --> 00:30:17,021
- Taxi!
- Don't tell me you're calling her.

430
00:30:17,188 --> 00:30:19,315
- I am. Yes, we do.
- We don't need her help.

431
00:30:19,816 --> 00:30:22,443
Hey, Erica, it's Patrick.
Sorry to wake you.

432
00:30:23,069 --> 00:30:24,821
Yep. Yep.

433
00:30:34,872 --> 00:30:37,041
Jan's got a meeting across town.
It should be fine.

434
00:30:47,593 --> 00:30:49,804
- Do you need help?
- Uh, yeah.

435
00:30:50,263 --> 00:30:53,015
No, actually. I...
I'm... I'm fine, thank you.

436
00:30:54,725 --> 00:30:56,477
- Do you know what you're looking for?
- Sort of.

437
00:30:57,436 --> 00:31:00,481
I always think of you as having a plan
but never admitting it.

438
00:31:01,482 --> 00:31:04,026
That's funny.
That's how I think of you.

439
00:31:07,280 --> 00:31:11,784
- How much longer?
- Not too much longer. Be patient.

440
00:31:14,412 --> 00:31:16,497
He's here. He's back.

441
00:31:26,632 --> 00:31:27,967
Light. Light, light, light, light.

442
00:32:03,544 --> 00:32:04,587
I think we're fine.

443
00:32:06,172 --> 00:32:08,424
Whew! That was close.

444
00:32:19,268 --> 00:32:23,356
- I'm sorry you didn't get your book.
- Oh, no. I got... I got my book.

445
00:32:24,232 --> 00:32:25,274
Right here.

446
00:32:27,318 --> 00:32:28,402
Let's go.

447
00:32:34,982 --> 00:32:38,068
- Excuse me, sir?
- Yeah, what do you got?

448
00:32:38,360 --> 00:32:42,156
Well, the C.I.A. analysts think the code
treats each book page like a grid

449
00:32:42,281 --> 00:32:44,158
so the first number
tells you what page number

450
00:32:44,283 --> 00:32:46,118
the second's the number
of letters across the bottom

451
00:32:46,243 --> 00:32:47,494
the third's the number
of letters up.

452
00:32:47,619 --> 00:32:49,246
Each three-number set
gives you one letter.

453
00:32:49,371 --> 00:32:52,249
- Yeah, how long to work it out?
- If they're right, 10 minutes.

454
00:32:52,458 --> 00:32:54,001
Call Cho as soon as you got it.

455
00:33:00,674 --> 00:33:01,925
FBI! Open up!

456
00:33:14,813 --> 00:33:15,856
"Clear!"

457
00:33:16,148 --> 00:33:17,191
Clear!

458
00:33:17,649 --> 00:33:18,692
"Clear!"

459
00:33:19,818 --> 00:33:20,903
"Clear upstairs!"

460
00:33:22,905 --> 00:33:24,490
Doesn't look like
anybody's been here for a while.

461
00:33:24,698 --> 00:33:26,492
You sure your people
got the right address?

462
00:33:28,785 --> 00:33:31,330
- Any sign of the chips upstairs?
- No. But they're looking.

463
00:33:32,623 --> 00:33:35,834
Maybe we want to bring in some techs
start tearing up the floorboards.

464
00:33:36,502 --> 00:33:37,628
They could be anywhere.

465
00:33:42,424 --> 00:33:44,551
- Are the shades drawn upstairs?
- I think so.

466
00:33:45,761 --> 00:33:46,803
This one isn't.

467
00:34:00,734 --> 00:34:02,027
Guess this is the right address.

468
00:34:15,082 --> 00:34:17,125
That's great. What about the group
John Paul belonged to?

469
00:34:17,251 --> 00:34:18,293
Did we get anything on them?

470
00:34:18,961 --> 00:34:21,505
From what we can tell
most of the membership's in the Philippines.

471
00:34:21,755 --> 00:34:25,384
Pretty low level, barely on the radar
but we have our eyes on them now.

472
00:34:26,301 --> 00:34:28,971
Tell me, how did Jane know
which book it was?

473
00:34:29,263 --> 00:34:31,431
It had to have been a book
that Nemec had read recently

474
00:34:31,557 --> 00:34:33,267
so he looked for one
that had a cracked spine

475
00:34:33,392 --> 00:34:34,643
that didn't have any dust on it.

476
00:34:34,851 --> 00:34:37,354
Nice. Nemec in custody yet?

477
00:34:37,729 --> 00:34:39,606
The Beirut police
are arresting him now.

478
00:34:41,066 --> 00:34:42,943
You think you get away?!

479
00:34:44,069 --> 00:34:46,572
I'll find you, Erica!
I'll find you!

480
00:34:47,489 --> 00:34:51,618
I'll cut off your head and I'll spit
down your neck you little bitch!

481
00:35:05,549 --> 00:35:06,592
Jane?

482
00:35:08,302 --> 00:35:10,220
I was just helping Erica
for a moment.

483
00:35:10,679 --> 00:35:11,722
Helping her?

484
00:35:12,347 --> 00:35:16,143
Yes. Jan was furious.
It was very upsetting.

485
00:35:17,394 --> 00:35:18,437
Okay.

486
00:35:19,605 --> 00:35:22,357
A Beirut police officer will take you
into protective custody

487
00:35:22,482 --> 00:35:25,319
until the embassy can arrange for you
to be escorted back to the U.S.

488
00:35:25,819 --> 00:35:26,862
Okay. That's fine.

489
00:35:27,863 --> 00:35:30,866
- Jane, can I talk to you for a second?
- Sure.

490
00:35:35,037 --> 00:35:38,290
- Look, Lisbon, she's just very upset.
- Well, I don't want to talk about her.

491
00:35:38,457 --> 00:35:41,209
This is about you.
You lied about kissing her.

492
00:35:41,335 --> 00:35:42,377
I didn't lie.

493
00:35:42,502 --> 00:35:46,006
And you insisted on getting her help
even after I specifically asked you not to.

494
00:35:46,131 --> 00:35:48,675
I'm working with her.
That's what we're supposed to do, right?

495
00:35:48,800 --> 00:35:50,677
Ever since we got here,
you're talking about Erica

496
00:35:50,802 --> 00:35:52,471
trying to find ways
to spend time with her.

497
00:35:52,596 --> 00:35:55,724
Lisbon, there's no reason to get
emotional here. It's... it's... it's nothing.

498
00:35:55,849 --> 00:35:58,226
Whatever happened
between you two, it's not over.

499
00:35:58,644 --> 00:36:01,688
- Just admit it.
- That is not true. It's not.

500
00:36:03,148 --> 00:36:06,318
I need to bring Nemec in with the police.
You do what you want. I'm done.

501
00:36:24,711 --> 00:36:28,382
I guess this is the officer that's
supposed to take you into custody.

502
00:36:30,509 --> 00:36:32,969
- He's not going to, is he?
- No.

503
00:36:42,437 --> 00:36:46,900
- You always have every angle covered.
- Don't you?

504
00:36:48,694 --> 00:36:50,487
- It's here, isn't it?
- What?

505
00:36:50,779 --> 00:36:53,573
That thing you wouldn't tell me about...
the other reason you did this.

506
00:36:53,865 --> 00:36:55,242
I'm sorry. I don't know
what you're talking about.

507
00:36:55,492 --> 00:36:59,830
The 1.5 million euro Nemec received
for those passport chips.

508
00:37:00,330 --> 00:37:02,666
The money... it's in this room.

509
00:37:03,250 --> 00:37:04,292
You needn't deny it.

510
00:37:04,668 --> 00:37:06,378
You're the smartest man I ever met.

511
00:37:14,678 --> 00:37:16,471
- It's pretty, isn't it?
- Gorgeous.

512
00:37:17,013 --> 00:37:19,015
You took your chance
when the C.I.A. came to you

513
00:37:19,141 --> 00:37:20,267
about turning Nemec in.

514
00:37:20,392 --> 00:37:22,227
Then you had his courier killed

515
00:37:22,352 --> 00:37:24,020
and you made it look like
it was an accident.

516
00:37:24,563 --> 00:37:25,772
Why do you always think
the worst of me?

517
00:37:25,897 --> 00:37:30,777
And you thought that you could use me
to take care of Nemec and take his cash.

518
00:37:31,111 --> 00:37:34,030
After all, you'd already gotten
away from me once before.

519
00:37:34,906 --> 00:37:37,033
You never intended
to go back to the U.S.

520
00:37:37,951 --> 00:37:40,245
All you wanted was Nemec's money
and freedom.

521
00:37:40,495 --> 00:37:42,956
- Maybe you're not as smart as I thought.
- Why would you say that?

522
00:37:43,081 --> 00:37:45,208
- It wasn't just about the money.
- What else?

523
00:37:47,461 --> 00:37:48,754
I missed you, Patrick.

524
00:37:52,174 --> 00:37:55,093
I'm a professional in the field of romance
and relationships.

525
00:37:56,219 --> 00:37:59,473
And it pains me to admit it,
but in my professional opinion

526
00:37:59,598 --> 00:38:03,185
- you and Lisbon will not last as a couple.
- Why is that?

527
00:38:03,393 --> 00:38:05,228
Because you're drawn to each other
for the wrong reasons.

528
00:38:05,479 --> 00:38:08,648
You're attracted to her virtue, her honesty
and her goodness...

529
00:38:08,857 --> 00:38:10,066
things you think you lack.

530
00:38:11,359 --> 00:38:17,949
She's interested in you for your danger
your unpredictability, your transgressions.

531
00:38:19,493 --> 00:38:22,454
These superficial qualities
are alluring, even erotic

532
00:38:22,621 --> 00:38:24,372
but they don't make
for a lasting relationship.

533
00:38:24,539 --> 00:38:27,375
- And what does?
- Finding your soul mate...

534
00:38:29,294 --> 00:38:32,506
the person who sees the world
the way you do

535
00:38:32,923 --> 00:38:35,509
who laughs at the things
you laugh at and...

536
00:38:36,676 --> 00:38:38,220
wants the things that you want.

537
00:38:38,678 --> 00:38:40,555
- And that's you?
- Here I am.

538
00:38:47,145 --> 00:38:48,355
I love Lisbon.

539
00:38:49,314 --> 00:38:52,317
And I'm taking you
to the real police

540
00:38:52,442 --> 00:38:54,653
and they're gonna lock you up
for the rest of your life.

541
00:38:55,654 --> 00:38:56,863
Oh, Patrick.

542
00:38:58,740 --> 00:39:00,075
I tried.

543
00:39:05,664 --> 00:39:06,748
Goodbye.

544
00:39:14,381 --> 00:39:15,757
On your knees, Erica.

545
00:39:18,510 --> 00:39:19,678
Do it.

546
00:39:24,683 --> 00:39:26,643
Jane, you all right?

547
00:39:27,310 --> 00:39:30,605
Yeah. Yep.
I'm okay, just...

548
00:39:33,984 --> 00:39:36,111
Oh, just need to walk it off
a little bit.

549
00:39:44,703 --> 00:39:46,288
- "Lisbon sent this?"
- Yeah.

550
00:39:47,205 --> 00:39:49,332
- What's gonna happen to her?
- Abbott says the deal's off.

551
00:39:49,499 --> 00:39:51,167
When she gets back, she'll serve
out the rest of her sentence.

552
00:39:51,334 --> 00:39:52,377
Vega.

553
00:39:53,128 --> 00:39:54,546
- Come here.
- Yes, sir.

554
00:39:55,213 --> 00:39:56,256
Sit down.

555
00:40:02,304 --> 00:40:03,346
You did good work today.

556
00:40:04,347 --> 00:40:06,975
But Abbott says he never approved you
leaving the office. You said he did.

557
00:40:07,976 --> 00:40:09,019
Did you lie to me?

558
00:40:10,604 --> 00:40:12,522
- Sir, I di...
- Answer the question.

559
00:40:15,650 --> 00:40:17,652
Yes. I'm sorry.

560
00:40:18,320 --> 00:40:21,406
I know that...
I just got excited, I guess

561
00:40:21,531 --> 00:40:23,575
- and I thought that I'd be a lot more...
- That's not your call.

562
00:40:24,159 --> 00:40:25,952
Now, I can't work with someone
I can't trust.

563
00:40:26,912 --> 00:40:29,247
- You can trust me.
- No, I can't.

564
00:40:30,457 --> 00:40:31,833
You're not going out
in the field with me again.

565
00:40:32,167 --> 00:40:34,002
- For how long?
- We'll see.

566
00:40:40,467 --> 00:40:41,509
Hey.

567
00:40:42,302 --> 00:40:43,345
You okay?

568
00:40:58,360 --> 00:40:59,402
What are we doing?

569
00:40:59,611 --> 00:41:01,821
- We need to get to the airport.
- Come on. You'll see.

570
00:41:01,947 --> 00:41:04,115
It says we have to be there
two hours early.

571
00:41:10,288 --> 00:41:11,873
Oh, my God. Jane!

572
00:41:14,250 --> 00:41:15,293
It's amazing.

573
00:41:17,587 --> 00:41:19,714
- You did this?
- Of course.

574
00:41:22,467 --> 00:41:23,885
- Please.
- Thank you.

575
00:41:24,886 --> 00:41:26,429
- And...
- What?

576
00:41:32,018 --> 00:41:34,980
Orange blossom ice cream.
You are good.

577
00:41:35,730 --> 00:41:37,315
- Dig in.
- Thank you.

578
00:41:42,696 --> 00:41:44,948
- What's that?
- Fireworks.

579
00:41:45,281 --> 00:41:46,324
Fireworks?

580
00:41:47,117 --> 00:41:48,994
It's the end of Ramadan.
They're celebrating.

581
00:41:50,120 --> 00:41:51,538
Fireworks. Beautiful.

582
00:41:51,830 --> 00:41:55,166
Yeah. It is beautiful.

583
00:41:59,004 --> 00:42:01,631
- You thought it was a bomb, didn't you?
- No, I didn't.

584
00:42:02,924 --> 00:42:04,676
- Yes, you did.
- I did not.

585
00:42:05,593 --> 00:42:06,636
You did.

586
00:42:06,970 --> 00:42:09,139
You know, this ice cream
is only just okay.

587
00:42:09,514 --> 00:42:10,890
Don't take it out
on the ice cream.

588
00:42:11,307 --> 00:42:15,228
I'm just saying, it's a long way to go
for an okay bowl of ice cream.

589
00:42:15,353 --> 00:42:18,606
As they say, you can take
the girl out of Chicago

590
00:42:18,732 --> 00:42:22,485
but you can't take the Chicago
out of the girl.
